---
import BaseLayout from "astro-theme-university/layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Card from "astro-theme-university/components/Card.astro";
import CardGrid from "astro-theme-university/components/CardGrid.astro";
import SemesterTimeline from "../components/SemesterTimeline.astro";
import { courseMeta } from "../course-config";
import { sessionLabels, siteConfig } from "../site-config";
import heroImage from "../assets/images/course-hero.svg";
import studioImage from "../assets/images/studio-card.svg";
import assessmentImage from "../assets/images/assessment-card.svg";
import peopleImage from "../assets/images/people-card.svg";
---

<BaseLayout
  title={`${courseMeta.code}: ${courseMeta.title}`}
  description={courseMeta.description}
  {...siteConfig}
  heroTitle={courseMeta.title}
  heroImage={heroImage}
  heroImageAlt="An architectural model of Kerrow Basin: gold buildings, planted blocks and a rust-coloured transit line, with three comparison bars."
>
  <p class="lead">{courseMeta.description}</p>

  <ul class="course-tags" aria-label="Course tags">
    {courseMeta.tags.map((tag) => <li>{tag}</li>)}
  </ul>

  <p class="kicker">One save file. Twelve weeks. No decision without a tradeoff.</p>

  <p class="lede">
    You will build something that works. Then a jury who was not in the room
    when you decided will ask what stopped working because of it.
  </p>

  <h2>What you will do</h2>
  <p>
    You will settle on a site and start your own save file in week one, and you
    will keep that file for the whole semester. Each week takes one planning
    problem —
    zoning, transit, infrastructure, budget, the public realm — and you will
    make the call in Cities: Skylines II, then defend it at crit against
    three named metrics: fiscal performance, mobility performance and
    livability. An improvement still needs an account of its costs. Learning to say what you
    traded, and why, is the actual syllabus.
  </p>
  <p>
    In Week 6 you will widen a bottleneck at Wickstead from two lanes to
    four. In the worked hypothetical, the reference route takes 11.2 minutes at the morning peak
    beforehand, and 8.9 the moment the wider road opens. Six in-game weeks
    later, the same route takes 10.7 minutes, likely because drivers who had
    been avoiding the old jam start taking the new road too. The widening
    genuinely helps — it just helps less than the first number says, and the
    studio makes you report both numbers instead of stopping at the one that
    flatters the decision. You will leave with a comprehensive plan for a
    district you can walk a critic through, screenshot by screenshot.
  </p>

  <h2>Why simulate it</h2>
  <p>
    "You can draw a transit spine with a ruler in an afternoon," Marisol
    Quaye, who convenes the studio, says. "What you cannot draw is anybody
    choosing to ride it. The model will not let you skip that, and I have
    never found a crit that could." That is the argument for building inside
    a simulation rather than on paper: it refuses to certify a plan just
    because it looks right. Week 1 sets a standing rule that holds for the
    rest of semester — a plan claiming all three metrics improved has not
    been read carefully — and every jury afterward spends its afternoon
    checking whether you read your own numbers as carefully as you are
    asking it to.
  </p>

  <h2>Who it is for</h2>
  <p>
    Second-year students who want their first studio — the crit format, the
    weekly deliverable, the discipline of defending a design decision out
    loud. No prior experience with the software is assumed; the studio
    teaches the tool alongside the theory. If you want a semester of debate
    about whether cities should exist at all, this isn't that course — every
    week here ends with something built.
  </p>

  <h2>The semester at a glance</h2>
  <p>
    Twelve teaching weeks, one lecture and one studio session each, a
    two-week break after Week 6. Nine of those sessions run as a desk crit;
    three are assessed, at the pin-up in Week 4, the jury in Week 9 and the
    final jury in Week 12.
  </p>
  <SemesterTimeline />

  <h2>Where to go next</h2>
  <CardGrid columns={2}>
    <Card title={sessionLabels.plural} href="/sessions/" image={studioImage} imageAlt="Three street plans pinned up for review, with a rust-coloured critique arrow.">
      <p>The weekly schedule and what happens in each studio.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="Assessment" href="/assessments/" image={assessmentImage} imageAlt="A street plan beside a gold checklist with three completed marks.">
      <p>What is marked, what it is worth, and when it is due.</p>
    </Card>
    <Card title="People" href="/people/" image={peopleImage} imageAlt="Five illustrated planners gather around a gold drafting table and a shared street plan.">
      <p>Who teaches the course and how to reach them.</p>
    </Card>
  </CardGrid>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .course-tags {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 0.5rem;
    margin-block: 0 1.25rem;
    padding: 0;
    list-style: none;
  }

  .course-tags li {
    padding: 0.25rem 0.65rem;
    border-radius: 999px;
    color: var(--at-text-secondary);
    background: var(--at-bg-alt);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }

  .lede {
    font-size: 1.15rem;
    max-width: 42rem;
  }

  /* The line that used to caption the home illustration. The illustration is
     now the page's hero band, which takes a title and nothing else, so the
     line stands on its own above the opening paragraph --- kept in the
     caption's voice and type so it still reads as a margin note rather than
     as prose. */
  .kicker {
    margin-block: 0 2rem;
    color: var(--at-text-muted);
    font-family: var(--at-font-mono);
    font-size: var(--at-font-size-xs);
  }
</style>
